window.QUIZZES = [{"id":"w6","title":"W6 — Supervised Learning: kNN, Naive Bayes, SVM","why_first":"4 in-class quiz topics (kernel, naive, zero likelihood, hinge)","qs":[{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A classmate says: “Naive Bayes is called naive because it ignores the class prior.” Which statement correctly fixes this?","options":["It is naive because it gives every class the same prior.","It is naive because it assumes the features (words) are independent of each other given the class.","It is naive because it only works with two classes.","It is naive because it uses word order to compute likelihoods."],"answer":1,"why":"The prior is used (the class's share of the training set, W6 p12). “Naive” = conditional independence: P(Message|Class) ≈ ∏ P(Word|Class), even though word order matters in real language.","wrong":{"0":"Priors are estimated from training proportions, not set equal.","2":"NB handles any number of classes.","3":"The opposite — it ignores word order."},"ref":"W6 p11, p14","anchor":"w6-naive","topic":"Naive Bayes: meaning of “naive”","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":31},{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"A new message contains the word “refund”, which never appeared in any VIP training post. (a) What happens to the VIP likelihood without any fix, and why? (b) How does the standard fix work?","model":"(a) P(refund | VIP) = 0/200 = 0. The likelihood is a PRODUCT of word probabilities, so one zero makes the whole VIP likelihood 0 — VIP becomes impossible no matter how strong the other words are. (b) Laplace smoothing: add one fictional occurrence to every category’s count, e.g. (count + 1)/(total + V), so no probability is ever 0.","ref":"W6 p13","anchor":"w6-laplace","topic":"Laplace smoothing / zero likelihood","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":32},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"kNN with Euclidean distance. Query point (2, 2). Labeled points: P1 (5, 6) class A, P2 (2, 5) class B, P3 (1, 1) class B, P4 (4, 2) class A. What is the prediction for k = 3?","options":["Class A","Class B","Tie — cannot decide","Class A because P1 is farthest"],"answer":1,"why":"Distances: P3 = √2 ≈ 1.41, P4 = 2, P2 = 3, P1 = 5. The 3 nearest are P3 (B), P4 (A), P2 (B) → majority vote = B.","wrong":{"0":"Only one of the 3 nearest (P4) is A.","2":"With k = 3 there is no tie (2 vs 1). Ties happen with even k — that’s why odd k is preferred.","3":"Farthest points are not used at all."},"ref":"W6 p6–p8","anchor":"w6-knn","topic":"kNN distance + majority vote","fig":null,"n":3,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":31},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"After training an SVM, you delete one training point that lies far from the hyperplane on the correct side (outside the margin). What happens to the hyperplane?","options":["It moves toward the deleted point.","It does not change.","The margin becomes wider.","The kernel must be changed."],"answer":1,"why":"Only support vectors (points on/inside the margin or misclassified) determine the hyperplane. A far, correctly classified point has no influence.","wrong":{"0":"Non-support vectors do not pull the boundary.","2":"The margin is set by the support vectors, which are unchanged.","3":"The kernel choice is unrelated."},"ref":"W6 p16–p17","anchor":"w6-svm","topic":"Support vectors","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":32},{"style":2,"type":"short","prompt":"The plot shows the SVM penalty as a function of how far an instance is into the margin. Point X sits on the flat part, point Y on the rising part. For each: is it penalized, and is it a support vector?","model":"X (flat part): penalty 0 — correctly classified and outside the margin; it does not influence the hyperplane (not a support vector). Y (rising part): positive penalty that grows linearly with its distance into the margin — it is inside the margin or misclassified, so it is a support vector and pushes the hyperplane.","ref":"W6 p19–p20","anchor":"fig-hinge","topic":"Reading the hinge-loss figure","fig":"q_hinge","n":5,"style_name":"Figure reading","pdf_page":67},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Hinge loss = max(0, 1 − y·f(x)) with y ∈ {−1, +1}. For an instance with y = −1 and f(x) = 0.3, what is the loss?","options":["0","0.7","1.3","−0.3"],"answer":2,"why":"y·f(x) = (−1)(0.3) = −0.3, so 1 − (−0.3) = 1.3 > 0 → loss 1.3. The instance is on the wrong side (f(x) > 0 but class is −1).","wrong":{"0":"Loss is 0 only when y·f(x) ≥ 1.","1":"That is 1 − 0.3: you forgot the sign of y.","3":"Hinge loss is never negative (max with 0)."},"ref":"W6 p19 (formula from review file)","anchor":"w6-svmformula","topic":"Hinge loss calculation","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":34},{"style":1,"type":"short","prompt":"Two features x and y cannot be separated by a straight line. Explain what the kernel trick does here, and how many dimensions a degree-2 polynomial kernel maps (x, y) into.","model":"The kernel trick maps the data to a higher-dimensional space where a linear hyperplane CAN separate the classes; a linear boundary there is a non-linear boundary in the original space, computed efficiently by the kernel. Degree-2 polynomial on two features → 6 dimensions: x², xy, y², x, y, 1.","ref":"W6 p15, p18","anchor":"w6-kernel","topic":"Kernel trick","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":32},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Features: annual income (dollars, 20,000–200,000) and age (18–80). Which model is MOST hurt if you skip feature scaling?","options":["Decision tree","k-nearest neighbors","Naive Bayes on categorical words","Random forest"],"answer":1,"why":"kNN adds raw differences inside the distance, so income (huge units) dominates age. Trees split one feature at a time on a threshold, so scale does not matter.","wrong":{"0":"Trees compare a single feature to a threshold — rescaling only moves the threshold.","2":"Category counts have no scale.","3":"A forest is many trees — same reason as trees."},"ref":"W6 p6; W8 p13","anchor":"w6-why","topic":"kNN needs scaling","fig":null,"n":8,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":34}]},{"id":"w4","title":"W4 — Regression (linear, polynomial, regularized, logistic)","why_first":"3 in-class quiz topics (n+1 points, k = 1/3/6, L1 vs L2 figure)","qs":[{"style":1,"type":"short","prompt":"You have 8 training points. What is the lowest polynomial degree guaranteed to fit all 8 exactly, and why is that a red flag rather than good news?","model":"Degree 7 (it has 8 coefficients w0…w7 → 8 equations, 8 unknowns → exact solution). Zero training error happens even if the data is pure noise, so it tells you nothing about test error; the model memorizes noise = overfitting (deck: ‘a degree-N polynomial exactly fits N+1 data points’).","ref":"W4 p31","anchor":"qp-n1","topic":"Overfitting: N+1 points","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":78},{"style":2,"type":"mcq","prompt":"The plot shows two error curves against polynomial degree. Which statement is correct?","options":["A is test error; best degree is 9.","A is training error; B is test error; best degree is about 4.","B is training error because it is U-shaped.","Both curves say degree 1 is best."],"answer":1,"why":"Training error keeps falling as complexity grows (A). Test error is U-shaped (B): high on the left (underfit), rising on the right (overfit). Pick the minimum of B ≈ degree 4.","wrong":{"0":"A keeps decreasing — that is the training-error signature; degree 9 overfits.","2":"The U-shape is the TEST error signature.","3":"Degree 1 has high error on both curves (underfit)."},"ref":"W4 p31, p37; W5 p6","anchor":"fig-complexity","topic":"Train vs test error curves","fig":"q_errcurves","n":2,"style_name":"Figure reading","pdf_page":69},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"You have 200 features but believe only about 10 really matter. Which regularizer is the better first choice?","options":["Ridge, because it shrinks all weights evenly","LASSO, because the L1 penalty can set weights exactly to 0 (feature selection)","No regularization, because more features always help","Ridge, because it removes irrelevant features"],"answer":1,"why":"LASSO’s |w| penalty drives some weights to exactly 0, performing feature selection and giving a sparse, interpretable model.","wrong":{"0":"Ridge shrinks but rarely zeroes weights — the 190 useless features stay in.","2":"Too many parameters relative to data → overfitting.","3":"That describes Lasso, not Ridge."},"ref":"W4 p45, p48","anchor":"w4-lasso","topic":"Lasso vs Ridge choice","fig":null,"n":3,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":21},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Gradient descent on f(w) = (w − 3)², starting at w₀ = 0, learning rate η = 0.25. What are w₁ and w₂?","options":["w₁ = 1.5, w₂ = 2.25","w₁ = −1.5, w₂ = −2.25","w₁ = 6, w₂ = 3","w₁ = 0.75, w₂ = 1.5"],"answer":0,"why":"f′(w) = 2(w − 3). f′(0) = −6 → w₁ = 0 − 0.25(−6) = 1.5. f′(1.5) = −3 → w₂ = 1.5 − 0.25(−3) = 2.25. Moving toward the minimum at 3.","wrong":{"1":"That uses a PLUS sign (moving uphill).","2":"Forgot to multiply by η.","3":"Used f′ = w − 3 (forgot the 2)."},"ref":"W4 p15","anchor":"w4-gdrule","topic":"Gradient descent step (minus sign)","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":22},{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"In the deck’s Ridge example, increasing α from 1 to 100 shrinks the slope from 570.63 to 95.90. What happens to bias and variance, and why must the features be standardized before using Ridge?","model":"Larger α → weights shrink toward 0 → simpler model → variance ↓, bias ↑ (the bias–variance tradeoff is controlled by α). The penalty is on weight size, and weight size depends on each feature’s units, so without standardization features measured in small units get unfairly penalized.","ref":"W4 p41, p44, p48","anchor":"w4-ridge","topic":"Ridge: effect of α","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":21},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"In logistic regression, the log-odds ln(p / (1 − p)) at some x equals ln 3. What is p?","options":["0.33","0.5","0.75","3"],"answer":2,"why":"Odds = p/(1 − p) = 3 → p = 3/(1 + 3) = 0.75.","wrong":{"0":"That would be odds = 1/2.","1":"p = 0.5 when log-odds = 0.","3":"3 is the odds, not a probability (probabilities ≤ 1)."},"ref":"W4 p59–p60","anchor":"w4-logformulas","topic":"Odds ↔ probability","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":23},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"MAP estimation of logistic regression with a Gaussian prior w ~ N(0, σ²) is equivalent to:","options":["MLE with no penalty","MLE plus an L2 penalty λwᵀw (Ridge-like)","MLE plus an L1 penalty","A closed-form solution"],"answer":1,"why":"The deck’s MAP objective is Σ log(1 + e^(−y wᵀx)) + λwᵀw: the Gaussian prior adds an L2 penalty. MLE is MAP with an uninformative prior.","wrong":{"0":"That is MLE, i.e. MAP with an uninformative prior.","2":"An L1 penalty would come from a Laplace prior (not in deck).","3":"Neither MAP nor MLE has a closed form in general."},"ref":"W4 p62–p65","anchor":"w4-mle","topic":"MAP vs MLE","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":22},{"style":1,"type":"short","prompt":"Give two of the deck’s reasons why ordinary linear regression is a poor model for a 0/1 response.","model":"Any two of: (1) non-normal error terms; (2) non-constant error variance; (3) the response must satisfy 0 ≤ p ≤ 1 but a line goes below 0 and above 1. → Use logistic regression (sigmoid keeps p in (0, 1)).","ref":"W4 p53–p55","anchor":"w4-whynotlinear","topic":"Why not linear regression for classification","fig":null,"n":8,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":21}]},{"id":"w7","title":"W7 — Unsupervised Learning: K-Means, DBSCAN, PCA","why_first":"3 in-class quiz topics (K-Means convergence, PCA, scree plot)","qs":[{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"You run K-Means twice with different random initial centroids and get final objectives J = 412 and J = 389. Is the algorithm broken? Explain using how K-Means converges.","model":"Not broken. Each run converges because neither step can increase J (assign to nearest centroid; centroid = mean) and there are finitely many assignments — but it converges to a LOCAL optimum that depends on initialization. Keep the run with lower J (389) or use more restarts.","ref":"W7 p11, p17, p25","anchor":"qp-kmeans","topic":"K-Means convergence / local optimum","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":79},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"1-D K-Means with k = 2. Points: 1, 2, 6, 8, 9. Initial centroids c₁ = 1, c₂ = 6. After ONE assignment + update step, the centroids are:","options":["c₁ = 1.5, c₂ ≈ 7.67","c₁ = 1, c₂ = 6","c₁ = 3, c₂ = 8.5","c₁ = 1.5, c₂ = 8.5"],"answer":0,"why":"Assign: 1, 2 → c₁ (2 is 1 away from c₁ vs 4 from c₂); 6, 8, 9 → c₂. Update: c₁ = (1+2)/2 = 1.5; c₂ = (6+8+9)/3 = 23/3 ≈ 7.67. J drops from 14 to about 5.2.","wrong":{"1":"That skips the update step.","2":"Put 6 in cluster 1 — but 6 is exactly at c₂.","3":"Left 6 out of cluster 2."},"ref":"W7 p11–p12","anchor":"num-kmeans","topic":"One K-Means step","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":83},{"style":2,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Scree plot for PCA on 6 standardized features (values shown). How many components should you keep, and about what share of the variance do they explain?","options":["1 component, 48%","2 components, about 77%","4 components, 93%","6 components, 100% — always keep all"],"answer":1,"why":"The curve levels off after component 3 → keep the components to its left: 2. Share = (2.9 + 1.7)/6 = 4.6/6 ≈ 77% (eigenvalue ÷ #features).","wrong":{"0":"The drop from 1 to 2 is still steep — component 2 still carries a lot.","2":"Components 3–6 are on the flat part (little variance, mostly noise).","3":"PCA’s purpose is to use fewer factors."},"ref":"W7 p41–p42","anchor":"qp-scree","topic":"Reading a scree plot","fig":"q_scree","n":3,"style_name":"Figure reading","pdf_page":81},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Data: two interlocking crescent-shaped groups plus a few isolated noise points far away. Which method fits best?","options":["K-Means with k = 2","DBSCAN","PCA","K-Means with k = 3"],"answer":1,"why":"DBSCAN groups dense connected regions of arbitrary shape and leaves sparse points as outliers. K-Means assumes compact convex blobs and forces every point into a cluster.","wrong":{"0":"K-Means cannot follow non-convex shapes and is pulled by outliers.","2":"PCA reduces features; it does not cluster.","3":"Changing k does not fix the shape problem."},"ref":"W7 p25, p33–p34","anchor":"w7-dbscan","topic":"K-Means vs DBSCAN","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":37},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"In PCA, what does a component’s EIGENVECTOR tell you (as opposed to its eigenvalue)?","options":["How much variance the component explains","The coefficients of the original features that make up the component (its direction)","The number of clusters in the data","The p-value of the component"],"answer":1,"why":"Eigenvector = feature coefficients/direction of the component. Eigenvalue = explained variability.","wrong":{"0":"That is the eigenvalue.","2":"PCA does not count clusters.","3":"No p-values in PCA."},"ref":"W7 p41","anchor":"w7-eigen","topic":"Eigenvector vs eigenvalue in PCA","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":38},{"style":2,"type":"mcq","prompt":"The plot shows the K-Means objective J for k = 1…6. Which k does the elbow method suggest?","options":["k = 1","k = 2","k = 3","k = 6"],"answer":2,"why":"Big drops until k = 3 (620 → 180), then only small gains (180 → 150 → 135). The bend (elbow) is at k = 3.","wrong":{"0":"k = 1 has the highest J.","1":"The drop from 2 to 3 is still huge (440).","3":"J always falls as k grows; the smallest J is not the goal."},"ref":"W7 p19–p23","anchor":"w7-elbow","topic":"Elbow method","fig":"q_elbow","n":6,"style_name":"Figure reading","pdf_page":36},{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"DBSCAN with ε = 1 and min_samples = 4. Point A has 5 points in its ε-neighborhood. Point B has only 2, but lies inside A’s ε-neighborhood. Point C has 1 and is not within ε of any core point. Classify A, B, C.","model":"A = core point (≥ min_samples in its ε-neighborhood). B = border point (in a core point’s neighborhood but not core itself). C = outlier (neither core nor border).","ref":"W7 p35–p36","anchor":"w7-dbscan-terms","topic":"DBSCAN core / border / outlier","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":37},{"style":2,"type":"mcq","prompt":"In the dendrogram shown, how many clusters does the dashed threshold produce, and what happens if you raise the threshold above all links?","options":["2 clusters; raising gives 8","4 clusters; raising gives 1","3 clusters; raising gives 2","8 clusters; raising gives 4"],"answer":1,"why":"Count the vertical lines the threshold crosses: {A,B}, {C,D}, {E,F}, {G,H} → 4. Above the top link, everything is merged into 1 cluster.","wrong":{"0":"Count crossings, not merges.","2":"There are 4 crossings at that height.","3":"8 = all leaves (a threshold at height 0)."},"ref":"W7 p31–p32","anchor":"w7-dendro","topic":"Reading a dendrogram","fig":"q_dendro","n":8,"style_name":"Figure reading","pdf_page":37}]},{"id":"w5","title":"W5 — Model Performance Evaluation","why_first":"predicted numeric item (confusion-matrix metrics)","qs":[{"style":5,"type":"short","prompt":"Confusion matrix: TP = 30, FP = 10, FN = 20, TN = 140. Compute accuracy, precision, recall, and F1.","model":"Accuracy = (30 + 140)/200 = 0.85. Precision = 30/(30 + 10) = 0.75. Recall = 30/(30 + 20) = 0.60. F1 = 2·0.75·0.60/(0.75 + 0.60) = 0.90/1.35 ≈ 0.667.","ref":"W5 p20 (F1: review file)","anchor":"num-confusion","topic":"Confusion-matrix metrics","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":83},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"An email filter moves suspected spam out of the inbox. Losing a real email (a false positive) is very costly. Which metric should you prioritize?","options":["Recall","Precision","Accuracy","MAE"],"answer":1,"why":"Precision = TP/(TP + FP) is hurt directly by false positives: ‘when I say spam, am I right?’","wrong":{"0":"Recall penalizes misses (FN), i.e. spam reaching the inbox — less costly here.","2":"Accuracy hides the FP/FN balance, especially with imbalanced classes.","3":"MAE is a regression loss."},"ref":"W5 p20–p21","anchor":"w5-metrics","topic":"Precision vs recall choice","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":28},{"style":2,"type":"mcq","prompt":"ROC curves for three models A, B, C are shown. Which statement is correct?","options":["C is best because it is a straight line.","A is best (largest AUC); C is random guessing with AUC 0.5.","B is best because it is in the middle.","All have the same AUC."],"answer":1,"why":"The curve closest to the top-left corner has the largest area under it. The diagonal is a random classifier (AUC = 0.5).","wrong":{"0":"The diagonal = TPR equals FPR at every cutoff = random.","2":"B lies below A everywhere → smaller AUC.","3":"The areas clearly differ."},"ref":"W5 p22","anchor":"w5-roc","topic":"Reading ROC / AUC","fig":"q_roc","n":3,"style_name":"Figure reading","pdf_page":27},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"True class y = 1. Model A predicts p̂ = 0.9, model B predicts p̂ = 0.6. Both classify correctly at threshold 0.5. Which has the lower log loss, and what does accuracy miss?","options":["B; accuracy misses nothing","A (−ln 0.9 ≈ 0.11 vs −ln 0.6 ≈ 0.51); accuracy ignores confidence","They are equal because both are correct","A; but log loss is higher for confident predictions"],"answer":1,"why":"Log loss = −log(probability given to the true class). Higher probability on the truth → smaller loss. Accuracy only sees the thresholded label, so it scores both the same.","wrong":{"0":"B gave the truth less probability → larger loss.","2":"Log loss uses the probability, not just correctness.","3":"Confident CORRECT predictions get LOW log loss; confident WRONG ones get very high loss."},"ref":"W5 p18","anchor":"w5-logloss","topic":"Log loss vs accuracy","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":26},{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"Why must hyperparameters (e.g. polynomial degree, α) be chosen on validation data or by cross-validation and never on the test set?","model":"The test set must stay unseen so it gives an honest estimate of performance on new data. If you tune on it, the test set is effectively used for training (data leakage), and the reported test error becomes optimistic.","ref":"W5 p8–p9; W4 p30","anchor":"w5-split","topic":"Validation vs test set","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":26},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Switching from 5-fold to 20-fold cross-validation mainly causes:","options":["Less computation and more bias","Smaller validation folds, more variability in the estimate, and more computation","No change at all","Larger validation folds"],"answer":1,"why":"Larger k → smaller validation sets (more variability) and more models trained on larger folds (more computation). Smaller k → more bias.","wrong":{"0":"Computation goes UP with k; more bias belongs to small k.","2":"Both variance and cost change.","3":"Each fold is 1/k of the data — smaller."},"ref":"W5 p24","anchor":"w5-cv","topic":"Choice of k in k-fold CV","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":27},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Dart analogy: all the shots land in a tight group, but far from the bullseye. This model has:","options":["Low bias, low variance","High bias, low variance","Low bias, high variance","High bias, high variance"],"answer":1,"why":"Bias = average distance from the bullseye (far → high). Variance = distance of shots from one another (tight → low).","wrong":{"0":"It would be on the bullseye.","2":"That would be scattered shots centred on the bullseye.","3":"Shots would be both scattered and off-center."},"ref":"W5 p5","anchor":"w5-bias","topic":"Bias vs variance","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":26},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Prediction errors are 2, −2, 2, −6. Compute MAE and RMSE. Which reacts more to the −6?","options":["MAE = 3, RMSE ≈ 3.46; RMSE reacts more","MAE = 3, RMSE = 3; equal","MAE = −1, RMSE = 12","MAE = 12, RMSE ≈ 3.46; MAE reacts more"],"answer":0,"why":"MAE = (2 + 2 + 2 + 6)/4 = 3. MSE = (4 + 4 + 4 + 36)/4 = 12 → RMSE = √12 ≈ 3.46. Squaring makes the big error dominate, so RMSE is more outlier-sensitive.","wrong":{"1":"Squaring changes the result.","2":"MAE uses absolute values (never negative); 12 is MSE, not RMSE.","3":"12 is the sum of |errors|, not the mean."},"ref":"W5 p13–p14","anchor":"w5-mse","topic":"MAE vs RMSE","fig":null,"n":8,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":27}]},{"id":"w12","title":"W12 — Intro to NLP (incl. LDA)","why_first":"in-class quiz topic LDA (Latent Dirichlet Allocation)","qs":[{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"In Latent Dirichlet Allocation, a “topic” is best described as:","options":["A cluster of documents","A probability distribution over words","A single keyword chosen by a human","A labeled class for supervised learning"],"answer":1,"why":"Topic = distribution over words; each document = distribution (mixture) over topics. Topics are latent — inferred from observed words.","wrong":{"0":"Documents are MIXTURES of topics, not members of one cluster.","2":"Topics are learned, not hand-picked.","3":"LDA is unsupervised — no labels."},"ref":"W12 p11","anchor":"w12-lda","topic":"LDA: what a topic is","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":61},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"You increase LDA’s α (document–topic density) and keep β fixed. What changes?","options":["Each topic uses more words","Each document is made of more topics","The number of topics k doubles","Documents become single-topic"],"answer":1,"why":"Higher α → documents are made of more topics. Higher β → topics are made of more words.","wrong":{"0":"That is the effect of a higher β.","2":"k is chosen separately (step 1 of the algorithm).","3":"That would be a LOWER α."},"ref":"W12 p14","anchor":"w12-alpha","topic":"LDA α vs β","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":62},{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"In the LDA update, a word w in document d is being reassigned. Name the two probabilities the algorithm multiplies, and say in plain words what each one asks.","model":"P(t | d) — ‘does this document like topic t?’ (share of d’s other words currently in t). P(w | t) — ‘does topic t like this word?’ (how often w appears in t in the other documents). Update: P(w | t) = P(t | d) · P(w | t), repeated until the distributions converge.","ref":"W12 p13","anchor":"w12-lda-algo","topic":"LDA algorithm steps","fig":null,"n":3,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":62},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Which normalization maps “was” → “be” and “better” → “good”?","options":["Stemming","Lemmatization","Tokenization","TF-IDF"],"answer":1,"why":"Lemmatization uses linguistics to get the root word, so it handles irregular forms. Stemming only chops prefixes/suffixes from a list.","wrong":{"0":"Chopping suffixes cannot turn ‘was’ into ‘be’.","2":"Tokenization only splits text into words.","3":"TF-IDF weights words; it does not normalize them."},"ref":"W12 p10","anchor":"w12-stem","topic":"Stemming vs lemmatization","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":61},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a Transformer train faster than an RNN on long sentences?","options":["It has no parameters","It processes all words at once with self-attention instead of one at a time","It ignores word order completely","It uses n-grams"],"answer":1,"why":"Transformers eliminate sequential dependency → parallel processing; attention links words at any distance.","wrong":{"0":"Transformers have very many parameters.","2":"Position information is still used.","3":"N-grams are the traditional approach."},"ref":"W12 p24, p29","anchor":"w12-transformer","topic":"Transformer vs RNN","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":64},{"style":4,"type":"mcq","prompt":"In an LSTM, which gate removes information that is no longer important?","options":["Input gate","Output gate","Forget gate","Attention gate"],"answer":2,"why":"Forget gate removes unimportant info; input gate adds new important info; output gate decides what to send forward.","wrong":{"0":"The input gate ADDS information.","1":"The output gate chooses what to pass on.","3":"Attention belongs to Transformers, not LSTM gates."},"ref":"W12 p28","anchor":"w12-lstm","topic":"LSTM gates","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":64},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Bigram model. In a corpus, “the” occurs 8 times; “the cat” occurs 3 times and “the dog” once. What is P(cat | the)?","options":["3/4","3/8","1/8","3/11"],"answer":1,"why":"P(w_i | w_{i−1}) = count(the, cat)/count(the) = 3/8 (Markov assumption: depends only on the previous word).","wrong":{"0":"Divided by the cat+dog count (4), not by count(the).","2":"That is P(dog | the).","3":"Added counts that do not belong together."},"ref":"W12 p23 (formula: standard)","anchor":"w12-ngram","topic":"Bigram probability","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":63},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pairing of model and task is correct?","options":["GPT (decoder-only, left-to-right) → text generation; BERT (encoder-only, bidirectional) → sentiment / NER","BERT → text generation; GPT → named-entity recognition","BART is decoder-only","GPT is pre-trained with masked language modeling"],"answer":0,"why":"Deck table: BERT = encoder, bidirectional, MLM, sentiment/NER; GPT = decoder, unidirectional, autoregressive, generation; BART = encoder–decoder, span corruption, translation/summarization.","wrong":{"1":"Swapped.","2":"BART is encoder–decoder.","3":"MLM is BERT’s objective; GPT is autoregressive."},"ref":"W12 p19","anchor":"w12-bert","topic":"BERT vs GPT vs BART","fig":null,"n":8,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":62}]},{"id":"w11","title":"W11 — Deep Learning","why_first":"new 30-slide deck; perceptron / gradient step predicted","qs":[{"style":5,"type":"short","prompt":"Perceptron with unit step (output 1 if z ≥ 0). w = (0.5, −1), b = 0, learning rate 0.2. Input x = (2, 1), true class 0. Do one update with the deck rule weight = weight + rate × error × input (error = actual − predicted). Give the new w, b and the new prediction.","model":"z = 0.5·2 + (−1)·1 + 0 = 0 → predicted 1. Error = 0 − 1 = −1. w₁ = 0.5 + 0.2(−1)(2) = 0.1; w₂ = −1 + 0.2(−1)(1) = −1.2; b = 0 + 0.2(−1)(1) = −0.2. New z = 0.2 − 1.2 − 0.2 = −1.2 < 0 → predicts 0 (now correct).","ref":"W11 p7","anchor":"num-perceptron","topic":"Perceptron update","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":85},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A slide writes the backprop update as weight = weight + r·∂L/∂w. If ∂L/∂w = +4 at the current weight, what would that update do to the loss?","options":["Decrease it — that is the correct rule","Increase it — it moves uphill; the correct rule uses a minus sign","Nothing, because r is small","It sets the weight to 4"],"answer":1,"why":"The gradient points uphill. Adding it increases the loss. Standard: w ← w − r·∂L/∂w (deck W11 p18 and W4 p15 use minus; W11 p11’s plus is likely a typo).","wrong":{"0":"Moving with a positive slope raises L.","2":"Any step uphill increases the loss.","3":"The update is a step, not an assignment to the gradient."},"ref":"W11 p11 vs p18","anchor":"w11-update","topic":"Gradient update sign","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":58},{"style":2,"type":"mcq","prompt":"The loss curves show training loss still falling while validation loss starts rising after about epoch 20. Diagnosis and a deck-supported remedy?","options":["Underfitting — add more epochs","Overfitting — stop training earlier / use dropout or regularization","Perfect fit — keep training","Learning rate too small — the loss is stuck"],"answer":1,"why":"Validation loss turning up while training loss decreases = memorizing the training data (overfitting). Dropout is the deck’s ‘improving training performance’ tool; stopping near the validation minimum also helps.","wrong":{"0":"Underfitting = both losses high.","2":"The gap is growing.","3":"Training loss is still decreasing well."},"ref":"W11 p13–p14, p17","anchor":"w11-losscurve","topic":"Reading loss curves","fig":"q_loss","n":3,"style_name":"Figure reading","pdf_page":56},{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"Explain why a deep network with sigmoid activations suffers from vanishing gradients. Use the maximum value of the sigmoid derivative.","model":"Backprop multiplies one activation derivative per layer (chain rule). The sigmoid derivative is at most 0.25, so after n layers the gradient is scaled by at most 0.25ⁿ (5 layers ≈ 0.001). Early layers get almost no update and stop learning. Fixes: ReLU, batch normalization; for sequences, LSTM.","ref":"W11 p20–p22","anchor":"w11-vanish","topic":"Vanishing gradients","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":56},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"1,000,000 training samples, mini-batch size 1,000. How many weight updates per epoch for full-batch GD, mini-batch GD, and SGD?","options":["1 / 1,000 / 1,000,000","1,000 / 1 / 1,000,000","1,000,000 / 1,000 / 1","1 / 1,000,000 / 1,000"],"answer":0,"why":"Full batch: one update after all n samples. Mini-batch: b = n / batch size = 1,000 updates. SGD: one update per sample = n.","wrong":{"1":"Full batch makes only ONE update per epoch.","2":"Reversed.","3":"Mini-batch and SGD swapped."},"ref":"W11 p23","anchor":"w11-minibatch","topic":"Mini-batch vs stochastic GD","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":56},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these models is GENERATIVE according to the deck?","options":["Support vector machine","Naive Bayes classifier","A decision boundary learned by a perceptron","k-NN"],"answer":1,"why":"Generative models estimate probability distributions of the data (can generate new data): some unsupervised models, naive Bayes, LLMs. SVM is the deck’s discriminative example.","wrong":{"0":"The deck’s example of a DISCRIMINATIVE model.","2":"A boundary = discriminative.","3":"kNN models no distribution."},"ref":"W11 p24","anchor":"w11-discgen","topic":"Generative vs discriminative","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":57},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Chain rule: ŷ = w·z, L = (y − ŷ)². With y = 5, w = 1, z = 2 and learning rate 0.05, what is ∂L/∂w and the updated w?","options":["∂L/∂w = −12, new w = 1.6","∂L/∂w = 12, new w = 1.6","∂L/∂w = −6, new w = 1.3","∂L/∂w = −12, new w = 0.4"],"answer":0,"why":"ŷ = 2. ∂L/∂w = f′(ŷ)·g′(w) = −2(y − ŷ)·z = −2(3)(2) = −12. Update: w = 1 − 0.05(−12) = 1.6 (w goes up, ŷ moves toward 5).","wrong":{"1":"Sign of the gradient is wrong (prediction too LOW → negative gradient).","2":"Forgot the factor z = 2.","3":"Used a plus-sign update (moved the wrong way)."},"ref":"W11 p21","anchor":"w11-chainformula","topic":"Chain rule gradient","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":58},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a multilayer network need NON-linear activation functions?","options":["To make training faster","Because stacking linear layers is still one linear function, which can only draw linear boundaries","Because outputs must be between 0 and 1","To avoid using a loss function"],"answer":1,"why":"A perceptron is good for linear boundaries only; complex data needs multilayer networks with non-linear activation.","wrong":{"0":"Speed is not the reason.","2":"ReLU outputs are not bounded by 1.","3":"Every network still needs a loss."},"ref":"W11 p8","anchor":"w11-nonlinear","topic":"Why non-linear activations","fig":null,"n":8,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":55}]},{"id":"w8","title":"W8 — Decision Trees and Random Forests","why_first":"predicted numeric item (Gini / entropy / information gain)","qs":[{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A node has 6 class-A and 2 class-B instances. A split sends 4 A to the left and 2 A + 2 B to the right. What is the weighted (overall) Gini index after the split?","options":["0.375","0.25","0.5","0"],"answer":1,"why":"Left: pure → 0. Right: 1 − 0.5² − 0.5² = 0.5. Weighted: (4/8)(0) + (4/8)(0.5) = 0.25. (Parent was 1 − 0.75² − 0.25² = 0.375, so the split helps.)","wrong":{"0":"That is the PARENT Gini.","2":"That is only the right child, unweighted.","3":"Only the left child is pure."},"ref":"W8 p16–p17","anchor":"w8-gini","topic":"Gini index calculation","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":42},{"style":5,"type":"short","prompt":"Same split as above. Using entropy, compute the information gain. (Use H(¼) ≈ 0.811 and H(½) = 1.)","model":"Parent H(6/8, 2/8) = H(¼) ≈ 0.811. Children: left 0, right 1. Weighted = ½·0 + ½·1 = 0.5. IG = 0.811 − 0.5 ≈ 0.311 bits. (Formula: not in deck — standard.)","ref":"W8 p16 (entropy: standard)","anchor":"num-entropy","topic":"Entropy / information gain","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":84},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the maximum possible Gini index for a node with 3 equally likely classes?","options":["1","2/3","1/3","0.5"],"answer":1,"why":"1 − 3·(1/3)² = 1 − 1/3 = 2/3. In general the max is 1 − 1/k (0.5 for two classes) — the deck’s ‘0 to 1’ is an approximation.","wrong":{"0":"1 is only approached with very many classes.","2":"That is Σp² , not 1 − Σp².","3":"That is the two-class maximum."},"ref":"W8 p16","anchor":"w8-gini","topic":"Gini range","fig":null,"n":3,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":42},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a random forest usually generalize better than one deep decision tree?","options":["Each tree uses all data and all features, so they agree more","Bootstrap samples + random feature subsets (m < M) decorrelate the trees, and voting/averaging reduces variance","Random forests never split on numeric features","Random forests are easier to interpret"],"answer":1,"why":"Double randomization reduces correlation among trees; combining many decorrelated trees cancels individual errors.","wrong":{"0":"Using the same data and features would make the trees nearly identical (no gain).","2":"They split on any feature type.","3":"A forest is LESS interpretable than one tree."},"ref":"W8 p20","anchor":"w8-rf","topic":"Random forest","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":42},{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"A regression tree is trained on houses priced $100k–$500k. You ask it to price a mansion much larger than any training house. What will it predict and why?","model":"At most the mean of the leaf the mansion falls into (around the highest training prices). Each leaf predicts the mean of its region, so trees do not extrapolate beyond the training range.","ref":"W8 p5, p13","anchor":"w8-why","topic":"Trees don’t extrapolate","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":43},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Compared with linear regression, which is TRUE of a regression tree according to the deck?","options":["It is heavily affected by outliers","Missing values can be treated as a separate class and it is not affected by outliers","It makes the statistical significance of each feature easy to measure","It fits linear data better"],"answer":1,"why":"Deck table: trees handle missing values as a separate class and are not affected by outliers; linear regression fits linear data better and gives significance easily.","wrong":{"0":"That is linear regression.","2":"That is linear regression.","3":"That is linear regression."},"ref":"W8 p6","anchor":"w8-vslinear","topic":"Regression tree vs linear regression","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":41},{"style":4,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is a stopping criterion used to keep CART from overfitting?","options":["Split until every leaf has exactly one instance","Set a minimum number of instances per leaf (or a maximum number of terminal nodes)","Always split on the feature with the highest Gini","Standardize all features"],"answer":1,"why":"Stopping criteria limit tree size: minimum instances in a leaf, or maximum terminal nodes.","wrong":{"0":"That is maximal overfitting.","2":"CART picks the LOWEST Gini.","3":"Trees do not need scaling."},"ref":"W8 p12, p19","anchor":"w8-stopping","topic":"Stopping criteria","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":41}]},{"id":"w9","title":"W9 — Ensemble Learning","why_first":"compare-and-judge favorite (bagging vs boosting)","qs":[{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Which ensemble method CANNOT fit its base models in parallel, and why?","options":["Bagging, because of bootstrapping","Boosting, because each base model needs the instance weights updated by the previous one","Stacking, because of the meta model","Random forest, because of random features"],"answer":1,"why":"Boosting is sequential: it updates instance weights after each base model to emphasize the poorly predicted instances.","wrong":{"0":"Bootstrap samples are independent → parallel.","2":"Stacking’s base models are fitted independently; only the meta model comes after.","3":"Trees are independent → parallel."},"ref":"W9 p4–p5, p7","anchor":"w9-seqpar","topic":"Sequential vs parallel ensembles","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":45},{"style":5,"type":"short","prompt":"AdaBoost: 10 instances, equal weights 1/10. The first base model misclassifies 2 of them. Using the deck’s rule (ε = fraction wrong; multiply misclassified weights by √((1−ε)/ε), divide correct ones by it, then normalize), give the new normalized weight of a misclassified and of a correct instance.","model":"ε = 2/10 = 0.2 → factor √(0.8/0.2) = √4 = 2. Relative weights: misclassified 2 each, correct ½ each. Sum = 2·2 + 8·½ = 8. Normalized: misclassified 2/8 = 0.25 each; correct 0.5/8 = 0.0625 each (check: 0.5 + 0.5 = 1).","ref":"W9 p10–p11","anchor":"w9-adaformula","topic":"AdaBoost re-weighting","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":46},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Bootstrap sample drawn from instances {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}: 2, 2, 5, 1, 5, 6. Which instances are out-of-bag, and what are they used for?","options":["{3, 4} — to estimate the base model’s error like a validation set","{2, 5} — they are the most important","{1, 6} — they appear once","None — bootstrap uses every instance"],"answer":0,"why":"OOB = never drawn = {3, 4}. They were not used to train this base model, so predicting them is an honest error estimate.","wrong":{"1":"Those were drawn twice — they are in the bag.","2":"Drawn once still means in the bag.","3":"Sampling with replacement leaves some out."},"ref":"W9 p16","anchor":"w9-oob","topic":"Out-of-bag instances","fig":null,"n":3,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":45},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"In stacking for a CLASSIFICATION task, which meta model does the deck use?","options":["Linear regression","Logistic regression","k-means","Another bootstrap sample"],"answer":1,"why":"Classification → logistic regression meta model; regression → linear regression meta model.","wrong":{"0":"That is for regression tasks.","2":"k-means is unsupervised clustering.","3":"Stacking trains base models on the entire dataset, not bootstraps."},"ref":"W9 p18","anchor":"w9-stack","topic":"Stacking meta model","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":46},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Five base classifiers vote Yes, No, Yes, Yes, No; four base regressors predict 12, 15, 9, 20. What are the ensemble outputs?","options":["Yes (60%) and 14","No and 14","Yes and 20","Yes and 56"],"answer":0,"why":"Classification: majority vote (3 of 5 = 60% Yes). Regression: average (12 + 15 + 9 + 20)/4 = 56/4 = 14.","wrong":{"1":"Only 2 of 5 voted No.","2":"Ensembles average; they do not take the max.","3":"56 is the sum, not the average."},"ref":"W9 p6","anchor":"w9-defs","topic":"Combining predictions","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":45},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key difference between plain bagging of decision trees and a random forest?","options":["Random forests do not use bootstrap samples","Random forests also restrict each split to a random subset of features (m < M)","Bagging updates instance weights","Random forests use a meta model"],"answer":1,"why":"Random forest = bagging + random feature subsets, which decorrelates the trees further.","wrong":{"0":"Both use bootstrap samples.","2":"Weight updates are boosting.","3":"A meta model is stacking."},"ref":"W8 p20; W9 p12–p15","anchor":"w8-rf","topic":"Bagging vs random forest","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":42},{"style":4,"type":"mcq","prompt":"When does AdaBoost stop adding base models?","options":["After exactly 10 models","When the maximum number of base models is reached or the error no longer changes","When every instance has equal weight","Never — it runs forever"],"answer":1,"why":"AdaBoost has no fixed number of base models; it stops at the max or when the error stops changing (often early). In the deck’s hepatitis example it stopped once model 2 got everything right.","wrong":{"0":"No fixed number.","2":"Weights become UNequal as it runs.","3":"It has stopping rules."},"ref":"W9 p8–p10","anchor":"w9-adaboost","topic":"AdaBoost stopping","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":45}]},{"id":"w10","title":"W10 — Recommender Systems","why_first":"self-contained topic (not in Géron) — easy points if prepared","qs":[{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A brand-new user signs up and has no interaction history. What is this problem called, and what is the deck’s suggested solution?","options":["Serendipity — show random items","Cold start — query the user for more information (e.g. ask preferences, import contacts)","Scalability — use matrix factorization","Feature extraction — use neural networks"],"answer":1,"why":"Cold start = no informative history (new user, or naturally sparse interactions). Solution: ask the user.","wrong":{"0":"Serendipity = recommendations become repetitive.","2":"Scalability = millions of users/items.","3":"Feature extraction = too many items to label by hand."},"ref":"W10 p14","anchor":"w10-problems","topic":"Cold start","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":49},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"User vector u = (1, 0, 1). Item A = (1, 0, 1), item B = (1, 1, 1). Compare dot product and cosine similarity.","options":["Dot: A = 2, B = 2 (tie); cosine: A = 1, B ≈ 0.82 → cosine prefers A","Dot: A = 2, B = 3; cosine prefers B","Both measures prefer B","Dot: A = 1, B = 1"],"answer":0,"why":"Dot products: 1+0+1 = 2 for both. Cosine = dot/(|u||item|): A = 2/(√2·√2) = 1; B = 2/(√2·√3) ≈ 0.82. Cosine penalizes the ‘un-specific’ item B.","wrong":{"1":"u has a 0 in position 2, so B’s extra feature adds nothing to the dot product.","2":"Cosine divides by B’s larger length.","3":"Count the matching 1s: there are 2."},"ref":"W10 p18–p19","anchor":"w10-cos","topic":"Dot product vs cosine","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":50},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A = {x, y, z, w}, B = {y, z, v}. Compute Jaccard and Dice similarity.","options":["Jaccard = 0.4, Dice ≈ 0.57","Jaccard = 0.5, Dice = 0.4","Jaccard = 2, Dice = 4","Jaccard ≈ 0.57, Dice = 0.4"],"answer":0,"why":"|A ∩ B| = 2 ({y, z}); |A ∪ B| = 5. Jaccard = 2/5 = 0.4. Dice = 2·2/(4 + 3) = 4/7 ≈ 0.57.","wrong":{"1":"Union has 5 elements, not 4.","2":"These are counts, not ratios.","3":"Swapped (Dice ≥ Jaccard always)."},"ref":"W10 p45–p46","anchor":"w10-sims","topic":"Jaccard and Dice","fig":null,"n":3,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":51},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"There are N = 10 documents. The word “the” appears in all 10; the word “cold-start” appears in 1. Using log base 10, what are their IDF values?","options":["the: 1, cold-start: 0","the: 0, cold-start: 1","both 10","the: 10, cold-start: 1"],"answer":1,"why":"idf = log(N / #docs containing t): the → log(10/10) = log 1 = 0; cold-start → log(10/1) = 1. Rare words get more weight; stop words get zero.","wrong":{"0":"Reversed.","2":"IDF uses a ratio and a log.","3":"Forgot the log and the ratio."},"ref":"W10 p26","anchor":"w10-tfidf-formula","topic":"IDF calculation","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":51},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Word2Vec skip-gram is trained to:","options":["Predict the center word from its context words","Predict the context words from the center word","Factor the global co-occurrence matrix","Count words in a document"],"answer":1,"why":"Skip-gram: center → context, P(w in context | center). CBOW is the reverse; GloVe factors co-occurrence; BoW counts.","wrong":{"0":"That is CBOW.","2":"That is GloVe.","3":"That is bag of words."},"ref":"W10 p31–p36","anchor":"w10-w2v","topic":"Skip-gram vs CBOW","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":50},{"style":3,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A website helps people buy a car — purchases are rare and cars have many complex features. Which recommender type fits best?","options":["Collaborative filtering","Knowledge-based","Pure content-based on viewing history","Bag of words"],"answer":1,"why":"Knowledge-based: for sparse interactions and complex item features; query the user’s needs and apply domain knowledge (if user needs X, recommend Y).","wrong":{"0":"CF needs plenty of interaction history.","2":"Viewing history is too sparse/uninformative.","3":"BoW is a text representation, not a recommender type."},"ref":"W10 p10–p11","anchor":"w10-types","topic":"Choosing the recommender type","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Compare & judge","pdf_page":48},{"style":4,"type":"short","prompt":"In collaborative filtering with Pearson similarity, why do we subtract each user’s mean rating, and why only use items both users rated?","model":"Subtracting the mean removes each user’s rating bias (generous vs harsh raters), so we compare taste, not scale. The similarity needs complete vectors, but most ratings are missing, so we only use the co-rated items S_xy.","ref":"W10 p51, p61–p62","anchor":"w10-sims","topic":"Pearson similarity in CF","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"Mechanism (how)","pdf_page":51},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"In matrix factorization for recommendation, how is a missing rating M_ij predicted?","options":["As the average of all ratings","As the dot product of user i’s vector and item j’s vector","As the cosine of the two vectors’ angle only","As the number of users who rated item j"],"answer":1,"why":"Learn d-length vectors so ⟨user_i, item_j⟩ ≈ M_ij; user vector = preferences, item vector = features.","wrong":{"0":"That ignores who the user is.","2":"The dot product (not just the angle) is used.","3":"That is a popularity count."},"ref":"W10 p9","anchor":"w10-mf","topic":"Matrix factorization","fig":null,"n":8,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":49}]},{"id":"w2","title":"W2 — Math Review: Probability, Statistics, Linear Algebra","why_first":"foundation; light Bayes / probability numbers","qs":[{"style":5,"type":"short","prompt":"A disease affects 1% of people. A test detects it 90% of the time when present, and gives a false positive 10% of the time when absent. If a person tests positive, what is P(disease | positive)? (Illustrative numbers.)","model":"Prior 0.01; likelihood 0.9. Marginal P(+) = 0.9·0.01 + 0.1·0.99 = 0.009 + 0.099 = 0.108 (law of total probability). Posterior = 0.009/0.108 = 1/12 ≈ 0.083 — only about 8%, because the disease is rare.","ref":"W2 p22, p25","anchor":"w2-bayes","topic":"Bayes theorem with total probability","fig":null,"n":1,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":16},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"P(A) = 0.3 and P(B) = 0.4. If A and B are mutually exclusive, which is true?","options":["They are independent, so P(A ∩ B) = 0.12","P(A ∩ B) = 0 and they are dependent","P(A ∪ B) = 0.12","P(A | B) = 0.3"],"answer":1,"why":"Mutually exclusive → cannot both happen → P(A ∩ B) = 0. That makes them DEPENDENT (knowing B happened makes A impossible). If they were independent, P(A ∩ B) would be 0.12.","wrong":{"0":"Independence and mutual exclusivity are different.","2":"P(A ∪ B) = 0.3 + 0.4 − 0 = 0.7.","3":"P(A | B) = 0 because A cannot happen when B does."},"ref":"W2 p23–p24","anchor":"w2-indep","topic":"Independent vs mutually exclusive","fig":null,"n":2,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":14},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A fair coin is flipped 4 times. What is the probability of exactly 2 heads, and what are E[X] and Var[X] for the number of heads?","options":["3/8; E = 2; Var = 1","1/2; E = 2; Var = 2","1/4; E = 2; Var = 1","3/8; E = 4; Var = 1"],"answer":0,"why":"Binomial: C(4,2)·(½)²·(½)² = 6/16 = 3/8. E = np = 2; Var = npq = 4·½·½ = 1.","wrong":{"1":"Var = npq, not np.","2":"Forgot the C(4,2) = 6 orderings.","3":"E = np = 2, not n."},"ref":"W2 p27","anchor":"w2-formulas","topic":"Binomial distribution","fig":null,"n":3,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":15},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A distribution of house prices has positive skewness. What does that mean?","options":["An extended left tail (many very cheap houses)","An extended right tail (a few very expensive houses)","Heavy tails on both sides","It is perfectly symmetric"],"answer":1,"why":"Positive skewness = extended right tail; negative = extended left tail. Tail heaviness on both sides is kurtosis.","wrong":{"0":"That is negative skewness.","2":"That is high kurtosis.","3":"Symmetric means skewness ≈ 0."},"ref":"W2 p7","anchor":"w2-moments","topic":"Skewness","fig":null,"n":4,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":13},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Data: 2, 4, 6. What are the population variance and the sample variance?","options":["8/3 ≈ 2.67 and 4","4 and 8/3","8 and 4","2 and 2"],"answer":0,"why":"Mean = 4; squared deviations 4, 0, 4 → sum 8. Population: 8/n = 8/3. Sample: 8/(n − 1) = 8/2 = 4.","wrong":{"1":"Swapped: the sample version divides by the smaller n − 1, so it is larger.","2":"8 is the sum, not the variance.","3":"That is a standard deviation-like guess."},"ref":"W2 p8","anchor":"w2-formulas","topic":"Population vs sample variance","fig":null,"n":5,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":15},{"style":5,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A = [[3, 0], [0, 1]]. Which statement is correct?","options":["(1, 0) is an eigenvector with λ = 3, and A·(2, 1) = (6, 1)","(1, 1) is an eigenvector with λ = 3","A has no eigenvectors","A·(2, 1) = (3, 1)"],"answer":0,"why":"A·(1, 0) = (3, 0) = 3·(1, 0) → λ = 3 (also (0, 1) with λ = 1). Matrix–vector: row 1 · (2, 1) = 6; row 2 · (2, 1) = 1.","wrong":{"1":"A·(1, 1) = (3, 1), not a multiple of (1, 1).","2":"Diagonal matrices have the axis directions as eigenvectors.","3":"Row 1 gives 3·2 + 0·1 = 6."},"ref":"W2 p37, p46","anchor":"w2-eigen","topic":"Eigenvectors and matrix product","fig":null,"n":6,"style_name":"Light math","pdf_page":15},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"Which matrix CANNOT be inverted?","options":["[[2, 0], [0, 5]]","[[1, 2], [2, 4]]","[[1, 0], [0, 1]]","[[0, 1], [1, 0]]"],"answer":1,"why":"Row 2 = 2 × row 1 → linearly dependent (low rank) → not invertible. A matrix also cannot be inverted if it is not square.","wrong":{"0":"Diagonal with non-zero entries → invertible.","2":"Identity — its own inverse.","3":"A permutation (orthogonal) matrix: inverse = transpose."},"ref":"W2 p42","anchor":"w2-inverse","topic":"Invertibility","fig":null,"n":7,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":14},{"style":1,"type":"mcq","prompt":"A hypothesis test gives p-value = 0.01. Which interpretation matches the deck?","options":["There is a 1% chance the null hypothesis is true","If the null hypothesis were true, data this extreme (or more) would occur only 1% of the time","The effect size is 1%","The alternative hypothesis is 99% likely"],"answer":1,"why":"p-value = probability of the observed outcome or something more extreme, computed assuming the null is true. Lower p → more confidence in the alternative.","wrong":{"0":"A p-value is not the probability that the null is true.","2":"It says nothing about effect size.","3":"It is not a probability of a hypothesis."},"ref":"W2 p31","anchor":"w2-pvalue","topic":"p-value","fig":null,"n":8,"style_name":"What / why","pdf_page":14}]}];
